# SRG-APP-000096-WSR-000057  -  CAT II
# The web server must produce log records containing sufficient information
# to establish when (date and time) events occurred.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000096-WSR-000057
group:           V-206360
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
revised:         yes
cci:             CCI-000131
control_rev5:    AU-3

contribution:    Every record Splunk's web tier writes to web_access.log and
                 splunkd_access.log begins with a date and time that no setting suppresses;
                 what the application configures is that those logs are written.

governs:         web.conf - Configures the Splunk Web interface.

check:           web.conf [settings] log.access_file non_empty
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          web.conf [settings] log.access_file https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
# --- end vendor verification

external:        The timestamp itself is emitted unconditionally by the product at the
                 start of each record in both access logs, so that part of the objective
                 has no configuration surface and is confirmed by inspecting a written
                 record.

citation:        SCOPE and INVENTION. Removed props.conf [<spec>] TZ present. TZ is an
                 index-time setting that assigns a time zone to data Splunk ingests; it
                 does not govern what the web tier writes into its own access logs, which
                 is what this requirement covers. props.conf.spec also gives "Default:
                 empty string" and a documented fallback chain - a zone in the record's raw
                 text, then TZ, then the forwarder's zone, then "the timezone of the system
                 that is running splunkd" - so an unset TZ is a supported configuration and
                 requiring it would fail a correct instance. The condition also
                 contradicted the criterion's own statement that the timestamp is emitted
                 unconditionally.
finding:         No HTTP access log is named in web.conf, so the web tier writes no record
                 for the date and time of each event to appear on.
